# Puzzle activity — the SQLite store is generated at runtime, and holds people.
#
# `activity/data/daily.sqlite` has players, preferences, runs, rush_runs and
# submissions: real Discord ids, usernames, avatar URLs and guild ids. This
# repository is PUBLIC, so committing it publishes all of that — and because
# the store runs in WAL mode, `git add` on the main file captures a torn
# snapshot that leaks a stale slice of those players while missing the puzzle
# tables entirely. There is no version of tracking it that is both safe and
# useful. `VACUUM INTO` de-WALs it faithfully and redacts nothing; it is the
# right backup tool and the wrong export tool.
#
# The one exception is `activity/data/archive/` — see below.
activity/data/*.sqlite
activity/data/*.sqlite-*

# The puzzle database site (activity/puzzledb/): its own build output and its own env file.
# Both are matched by the generic rules above; named here so nothing about them depends on a
# template's rules. Its public SQLite is built in memory and never written, so no .sqlite rule.
activity/puzzledb/dist/
activity/puzzledb/.env

# The raw puzzle archive export. Decoded output lives in activity/data/puzzles.json.
tmp/

# Inputs to a job tracker this repository no longer carries: a real resume and
# H-1B research. Purged from history on 2026-09-02; never track this directory
# again. (activity/data/ is a different directory and stays tracked.)
/data/

# The answers, as a loose file. `bun run puzzles` writes these beside the
# tracked puzzles.json, which deliberately has none.
#
# The reasoning was that a solution sitting next to its puzzle in a public
# repository is a published answer key. That rule is now narrower than it was,
# by a decision rather than by drift: the club publishes the answers already —
# the website repository commits `solution_url` for every puzzle — so the
# archive database below carries solutions on purpose. What stays true is that
# the *game* never hands a player an answer mid-run, which is enforced by
# `PuzzlePrompt` in shared/puzzle.ts and not by this file.
#
# This entry stays because a second, untracked copy of the answers with no
# owner is still worth not having.
activity/data/solutions.json

# The tracked exception: the club's puzzle archive, as a file.
#
# `activity/data/archive/puzzles.sqlite` is built fresh by
# `bun run sync-archive --db ...`, which creates only ARCHIVE_SCHEMA — so it
# has never held a player table, and cannot. It exists so a clone, a CI job or
# another project has the puzzles without a spreadsheet round trip.
#
# It is safe for three reasons, and `activity/tests/tracked-archive.test.ts`
# asserts all of them rather than trusting them: it contains only
# archive_puzzles and archive_content_log; no column in it can name a person;
# and every row is unpublished, so the file cannot change what a player is
# served just by existing.
#
# Never put a -wal or -shm here, and never `cp` or `VACUUM INTO` the live
# database into this directory.
!activity/data/archive/
!activity/data/archive/puzzles.sqlite
activity/data/archive/*.sqlite-*

# Audit artefacts: generated reports, audit dumps and pre-change database
# backups. Local working notes, deliberately never committed — the tools that
# produce them (activity/tools/audit-archive.ts, sync-archive.ts) are tracked,
# their output is not.
/audit/

# Who could run /archive sync, back when it had an officer allowlist. Nothing
# reads it now — anyone may run the command, once every 10 minutes — but an old
# copy may still sit on a box, and it holds real Discord user ids, which this
# public repository must never carry: git history here is append-only — force
# pushes and branch deletion are refused for everyone, owners included — so a
# mistaken commit of somebody's id could not be taken back. Kept ignored so an
# old file is never committed.
puzzle-admins.json
